meta {
  name: 31 Transfer - Reject
  type: http
  seq: 31
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/accession/transfers/{{transferId}}/reject
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{businessAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "rejectionReason": "Damaged in Transit",
    "notes": "Tube cracked"
  }
}

tests {
  test("transition applied (or rejected by the state machine)", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.be.oneOf([200, 201, 422]);
  });
}

docs {
  Reject (§B.11.5) — **Received → Rejected**, and also **Accepted → Rejected**
  (reject-after-accept). `rejectionReason` is **required**. Origin branch is
  notified; the sample is not processed. For an INTERNAL transfer rejected from
  Accepted, the accept-time clone in the receiving branch is unwound (soft-deleted
  + its lab reports re-homed to origin) before the transfer is marked Rejected.

  **Bulk:** `POST /accession/transfers/bulk/reject` (and the other
  `transfers/bulk/<action>` — pick-up/receive/accept/repeat) take `{ ids: [...],
  ...fields, skipInvalid? }` and resolve to the structured result
  `{ succeeded: [<transfer>…], skipped: [{ id, accessionNo, reason }…] }`. With
  `skipInvalid: true` (the multi-select bar), transfers not in a legal state for
  the action are skipped and listed under `skipped` instead of failing the batch.

  **Cancelled order:** once the receiving branch has ACCEPTED its copy, the copy
  holds typed results, so it can no longer be sent back to the sender - refused
  with 422 `SAMPLE_ORDER_CANCELLED` (a group call with `skipInvalid` skips it with
  that reason) and nothing changes; Store then Discard stay open. Before Accept
  (transfer only Received) the receiving branch can still Reject / Repeat.
}
